      "math_academy_id": "q-314207",
      "sequence_position": 5,
      "problem": "Given that $f(x)={({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})}^{2},$ find ${f}^{′}(x).$\n\n${f}^{′}(x)=2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})($\n{{field-1}}\n$)$",
      "worked_solution": "Let $g(x)={x}^{4}-3{x}^{3}$ and $f(g(x))=g(x)^{2}.$ \n\nDifferentiating, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(g(x)) & =({g}^{2})^{′}=2g, \\\\ {g}^{′}(x) & =({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})^{′}=4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThen, using the chain rule, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{′}(x) & ={f}^{′}(g(x))\\cdot {g}^{′}(x) \\\\  & =2g\\cdot (4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}) \\\\  & =2({x}^{4}-3{x}^{3})(\\,4{x}^{3}-9{x}^{2}\\,).\\end{aligned}\n$$",
